WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.141
RTIC security is a layered embedded
engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.221 --> 00:00:05.968
Start by mapping device assets, external
interfaces, tasks,

3
00:00:06.048 --> 00:00:09.112
execution contexts, shared and local
resources, trust boundaries,

4
00:00:09.192 --> 00:00:10.460
and lifecycle assumptions.

5
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.344
The architecture should show how input can
influence resources and device behavior.

6
00:00:14.424 --> 00:00:16.748
Use RTIC structure to make concurrency
reviewable.

7
00:00:16.828 --> 00:00:18.994
Priorities should reflect time-critical
work.

8
00:00:19.074 --> 00:00:21.240
Locks and critical sections should stay
narrow.

9
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.852
Interrupt work should be bounded.

10
00:00:22.932 --> 00:00:24.781
DMA buffer ownership should be explicit.

11
00:00:24.861 --> 00:00:28.717
Memory, queues, and panic behavior should
have product-specific limits and recovery

12
00:00:28.797 --> 00:00:29.484
expectations.

13
00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:31.677
Treat protocol parsing as a security
boundary.

14
00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:33.923
Validate length, framing, fields, state,
mode,

15
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:36.591
and relationships before actions reach
sensitive logic.

16
00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:40.316
Convert valid messages into domain types
where possible so later tasks operate on

17
00:00:40.396 --> 00:00:42.192
reviewed meaning rather than raw bytes.

18
00:00:42.272 --> 00:00:44.227
Document hardware and unsafe assumptions.

19
00:00:44.307 --> 00:00:47.635
Review peripheral ownership, register
behavior, initialization order,

20
00:00:47.715 --> 00:00:49.722
interrupt interactions, and safe wrappers.

21
00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:51.968
Unsafe code should be small, justified,
tested,

22
00:00:52.048 --> 00:00:54.848
and protected by APIs that ordinary
callers can use correctly.

23
00:00:54.928 --> 00:00:56.354
Finally, harden the lifecycle.

24
00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:59.710
Protect secrets, updates, debug paths,
dependencies, build artifacts,

25
00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.854
signing materials, release evidence, and
maintenance procedures.

26
00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.047
Test timing, failure paths, input
boundaries,

27
00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:08.877
and lifecycle assumptions over time so
firmware remains reviewable after the first

28
00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:09.380
release.
